    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "Kimi25 has Day of Judgment in hand and reasons they should wait to board wipe, but the opponent has an Eddymurk Crab (5/5) AND a Sunderflock (5/5 flying) already on the battlefield. The opponent is casting Opt, not a creature - the creatures are already deployed. Meanwhile, Kimi25 is at 18 life facing 10 power of attackers (5 of which has flying). The correct play here is to use No More Lies or Spell Snare (not in hand) to counter the Opt - actually, countering Opt with No More Lies is wasteful. The real issue is that Kimi25 should be casting Day of Judgment THIS turn during their main phase rather than passing. However, the decision point is specifically about whether to counter an Opt on the stack. Countering Opt with No More Lies (spending 2 mana to counter a cantrip that costs 1) is reasonable value denial, preventing the opponent from finding answers to Day of Judgment. But declining is also defensible since they want mana open. The reasoning mentions Sunderflock as a '3/2 flyer' when it's actually a 5/5 flyer - the villain is confusing it with Tishana's Tidebinder. This misread of the board could lead to underestimating the threat level, though the decision to not counter Opt is not terrible in isolation.",
